"Spherical Steering: Geometry-Aware Activation Rotation for Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08169v1)\nciteturn31647view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view0\",\"lineno\":462}); Total lines: 533\nL439: Table 5: TruthfulQA results under zero-shot and 5-shot ICL settings on LLaMA-3.1-8B. Metrics are in percentage (%). Key insight: Spherical Steering achieves the best multiple-choice performance while simultaneously improving generation quality.\nL440: Setting  | Method  | Multiple-Choice  | Open-ended Generation\nL441: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL442:  | MC1  | MC2  | MC3  | Avg.  | TRUE  | INFO  | TRUE$\\times$INFO\nL443: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL444: Zero-shot  | Baseline  | 27.05  | 47.12  | 22.26  | 32.14  | 37.85  | 95.76  | 36.24\nL445:  | ITI (cite79†Li et al., 2023 )  | 28.03  | 48.22  | 23.28  | 33.18  | 38.10  | 94.78  | 35.57\nL446:  | CAA (cite80†Rimsky et al., 2024 )  | 28.52  | 49.09  | 24.04  | 33.88  | 40.13  | 97.17  | 38.99\nL447:  | SADI-HEAD (cite102†Wang et al., 2024 )  | 32.38  | 49.90  | 25.04  | 35.77  | 47.06  | 78.92  | 37.14\nL448:  | Spherical Steering (Ours)  | 37.19  | 57.45  | 29.93  | 41.52  | 75.44  | 71.56  | 53.98\nL449: 5-shot ICL  | Baseline (5-shot ICL)  | 30.48  | 49.33  | 23.50  | 34.44  | 54.78  | 82.68  | 45.29\nL450:  | ITI (cite79†Li et al., 2023 )  | 32.68  | 49.42  | 24.35  | 35.48  | 60.07  | 79.49  | 47.75\nL451:  | CAA (cite80†Rimsky et al., 2024 )  | 31.82  | 50.09  | 24.22  | 35.38  | 52.88  | 90.10  | 46.84\nL452:  | SADI-HEAD (cite102†Wang et al., 2024 )  | 32.68  | 50.10  | 25.13  | 35.97  | 66.51  | 67.06  | 44.60\nL453:  | Spherical Steering (Ours)  | 37.22  | 55.69  | 28.87  | 40.59  | 73.51  | 76.14  | 55.95\nL454: We also evaluate Spherical Steering on LLaMA-3.1-8B to verify that the accuracy–generation improvements in the main text are not specific to instruct-tuned models. Table cite139†5 reports zero-shot TruthfulQA results. Compared to activation addtion baselines (ITI/CAA) and SADI-HEAD, Spherical Steering delivers the strongest multiple-choice improvements (Avg) while also achieving the best open-ended quality measured by True$\\times$Info.\nL455: Notably, additive edits yield only modest gains on multiple-choice metrics and exhibit less favorable open-ended behavior, whereas norm-preserving rotation improves both axes more consistently.\nL456: We also test whether the intervention remains effective on top of prompt-based adaptation on this model. Under 5-shot ICL (Table cite139†5 ), Spherical Steering continues to provide clear gains over the ICL baseline and additive steering variants, improving multiple-choice accuracy while simultaneously increasing True$\\times$Info.\nL457: This shows that our rotation-based update composes well with in-context demonstrations: rather than trading off open-ended quality for likelihood-based accuracy, it strengthens both capabilities under the same prompting setup.\nL458: Overall, these additional results on LLaMA-3.1-8B further support our central claim that activation rotation is a stronger inference-time control primitive than activation addition, and that it remains effective when combined with few-shot prompting.\nL459: ## Appendix C Implementation Details\nL460: \nL461: We provide our implementation details here.\nL462: ### C.1 Datasets and Splits\nL463: \nL464: We evaluate our models on six datasets covering different domains of open-ended QA and multiple-choice tasks.\nL465: \nL466:   * •\nL467: Choice of Plausible Alternatives (COPA) (cite118†Gordon et al., 2012 ) is a causal reasoning benchmark. Each example provides a short premise and two candidate alternatives; the model must choose the alternative that is more plausibly related to the premise via a causal relation (typically cause→effect or effect→cause). Because the two options are minimally different and both are plausible on the surface, COPA probes whether a model can rely on commonsense causal structure rather than shallow lexical cues.\nL468: We randomly divide develop split (400 examples), and split the training/ validation set by $4:1$. Using the rest of 100 examples for the test set.\nL469: \nL470:   * •\nL471: StoryCloze (cite119†Mostafazadeh et al., 2016 ) evaluates narrative understanding and script learning. Each instance contains a four-sentence story context and two candidate endings; the task is to select the ending that best completes the story in a coherent and commonsense way. Success requires tracking entities, events, and implicit temporal/causal consistency, making it a useful probe of higher-level discourse reasoning.\nL472: We use the dataset’s provided train/eval partitions, using train set as our developing set, and split the training/ validation set by $4:1$. Using the full official eval set (1511 examples) for evaluation.\nL473: \nL474:   * •\nL475: Massive Multitask Language Understanding (MMLU) (cite121†Hendrycks et al., 2020 ) measures broad factual knowledge and reasoning acquired during pretraining. It covers 57 subjects spanning STEM, humanities, social sciences, and professional domains. The task format is multiple-choice, and the benchmark is widely used to assess general knowledge breadth as well as domain robustness under diverse question styles and difficulty levels.\nL476: We divide the dataset into 4 categories, STEM, Humanities, Social Sciences and Others. 500 examples per category for the $4:1$ training / validation split. 200 examples per category for evaluation.\nL477: \nL478:   * •\nL479: BoolQ (cite120†Clark et al., 2019 ) is a yes/no question answering dataset built from naturally occurring queries. Each example consists of a short passage and a question, and the model predicts a binary answer. BoolQ stresses reading comprehension and inference under partial evidence, since the passage often contains relevant information implicitly rather than via explicit lexical overlap with the question.\nL480: We randomly sample 1000 examples from official train set as developing set, and split the training / validation set by $4:1$. Using the full official validation set (3270 examples) for evaluation.\nL481: \nL482:   * •\nL483: WinoGrande (cite122†Sakaguchi et al., 2020 ) is a binary fill-in-the-blank commonsense reasoning benchmark. Each example presents a sentence with a blank and two candidate entities; the goal is to choose the option that makes the sentence logically consistent. The dataset is designed to reduce annotation artifacts and thus more directly test commonsense coreference and pragmatic reasoning.\nL484: We randomly sample 1000 examples from official train set as developing set, and split the training / validation set by $4:1$. Using the full official validation set (1267 examples) for evaluation.\nL485: \nL486:   * •\nL487: TruthfulQA (cite117†Lin et al., 2022 ) evaluates whether a language model produces truthful and non-misleading responses, particularly for questions that trigger common misconceptions. It contains 817 questions across 38 categories (e.g., health, law, finance, politics). TruthfulQA provides both a multiple-choice track and an open-ended generation track.\nL488: We use 2 folds cross-validation over the 817 questions. For each fold, randomly split the training / validation set by $4:1$, building prototypes using the training set only.\nL489: ### C.2 Evaluation Metrics in Detail\nL490: In Table cite93†1 we follow the official TruthfulQA (cite117†Lin et al., 2022 ) multiple-choice evaluation by scoring each reference answer with its conditional log-likelihood (sum of token log-probabilities). MC1 reports whether the best correct answer outranks all incorrect answers; MC3 reports the fraction of correct answers that outrank the top incorrect answer; and MC2 reports the normalized probability mass assigned to correct answers after exponentiating and normalizing scores over all candidates.\nL491: For open-ended generation, we employ two pre-trained judge models to judge the truthfulness^{1}^{1} 1 https://huggingface.co/allenai/truthfulqa-truth-judge-llama2-7B and informativeness^{2}^{2} 2 https://huggingface.co/allenai/truthfulqa-info-judge-llama2-7B denoted as TRUE (%) and INFO (%), respectively. Following the prior work (cite79†Li et al., 2023 ), we use their product TRUE$\\times$INFO (%) as the primary metric.\nL492: For other multiple-choice evaluation benchmarks, we consider COPA (cite118†Gordon et al., 2012 ), StoryCloze (cite119†Mostafazadeh et al., 2016 ), BoolQ (cite120†Clark et al., 2019 ), MMLU (cite121†Hendrycks et al., 2020 ), and WinoGrande (cite122†Sakaguchi et al., 2020 ), covering answer options ranging from 2-way to 4-way.\nL493: To evaluate a model on each multiple-choice question, we use the same likelihood-based scoring: we compute the conditional log-likelihood of each candidate option under the task prompt and report accuracy.\nL494: ### C.3 Supplementary Settings for Rank Analysis\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLinearization Explains Fine-Tuning in Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08239v1)\nciteturn31647view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view2\",\"lineno\":82}); Total lines: 810\nL64: 1 of [cite49†17 ]). In our work, however, by introducing an explicit inductive bias for fine-tuning, which is essentially a weight decay toward the original model parameter, we induce lazy training. Consequently, the fine-tuning performance can be approximated by the linearized model [cite50†18 , cite41†9 ]. The evolution of the model is described by the neural tangent kernel (NTK), which stays constant during fine-tuning.\nL65: This property makes it possible to study the generalization properties of a fine-tuned model based on the spectral properties of the NTK. Specifically, our main contributions are:\nL66:   * •\nL67: \nL68: We show that having an explicit inductive bias toward the pretrained model results in linearized fine-tuning; more precisely, it brings fine-tuned and linearized models closer.\nL69: \nL70:   * •\nL71: \nL72: Leveraging the linearization of fine-tuning, we formulate the fine-tuning problem as a neural tangent kernel regression and use the eigenvalue decomposition of the neural tangent kernel to derive bounds on the empirical risk of the end result of fine-tuning, prior to its execution.\nL73: \nL74:   * •\nL75: We provide bounds on the spectral perturbation on the NTK when a set of trainable parameters is added to fine-tuning. In particular, to the best of our knowledge, we present the first spectrum perturbation results due to layer selection and introduce an algorithm that provides new insights to guide fine-tuning design.\nL76: \nL77:   * •\nL78: Through extensive experiments, we validate our theoretical results. We evaluate the condition number of NTK as an at-initialization metric to anticipate the performance of LoRA before training. Even though our experiments focus on LoRA, the technical tools we introduce could be equally used in the context of other PEFT methods.\nL79: The paper is organized as follows. In §cite7†2 , we present the problem formulation and notation. In §cite8†3 , we detail the linearization framework. In §cite9†4 , we give an NTK regression formulation for the linearized fine-tuning and related performance to the kernel’s spectrum. In §cite10†5 , we show how layer choices affect this spectrum. We then validate our findings through experiments in §cite11†6 and conclude in §cite15†7 .\nL80: ## 2 Problem Formulation\nL81: In the fine-tuning problem, we are given a pretrained model $f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\cdot)$, a target task dataset $\\mathcal{D}_{T}={(\\mathbf{x}_{i},\\mathbf{Y}_{i})}_{i=1}^{n}$ for the downstream task, and a loss function $\\mathcal{L}(\\cdot,\\cdot):\\mathbb{R}\\times\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$. The most basic fine-tuning solution is to minimize the target empirical risk via gradient descent, when initialized at $f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\cdot)$. This implicitly finds solutions close to the pretrained model.\nL82: In this paper, we make this explicit via an inductive bias toward the pretrained model. We denote the regularized fine-tuned model by $f_{\\bm{\\theta}^{\\star}}(\\cdot):\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\to\\mathbb{R}$, and we define it as minimizing the regularized empirical risk.\nL83:  | $$\\bm{\\theta}^{\\star}=\\;\\underset{\\bm{\\theta}}{\\textrm{minimize}}\\ \\widetilde{\\mathcal{R}}(\\bm{\\theta})+\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\|\\bm{\\theta}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\|^{2}_{2},\\quad\\textrm{where}$$  |  | (1)\nL84:  | $$\\widetilde{\\mathcal{R}}(\\bm{\\theta})=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\mathcal{L}(f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{x}_{i}),\\mathbf{y}_{i}).$$  |  | (2)\nL85: $\\bm{\\theta}$ denotes the trainable fine-tuning parameters, $\\bm{\\theta}_{0}$ denotes the parameters of the pretrained model, $\\widetilde{\\mathcal{R}}(\\bm{\\theta})$ the original objective function, and $\\lambda$ the regularization strength hyperparameter. When $\\bm{\\theta}_{0}=0$, (cite51†1 ) reduces to an ordinary weight decay. This regularization reduces the deviation between the fine-tuned and pretrained models, and is a simple instance of proximal methods often used in fine-tuning.\nL86: We assume $\\mathcal{L}(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ is the squared loss, and thus the risk is the MSE. By representing $\\mathcal{D}_{T}={(\\mathbf{x}_{i},\\mathbf{y}_{i})}_{i=1}^{n}$ as $\\mathbf{X}=\\left[\\mathbf{x}_{1},\\mathbf{x}_{2},\\ldots,\\mathbf{x}_{n}\\right]^{\\top}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times d}$, and $\\mathbf{Y}=\\left[\\mathbf{y}_{1},\\mathbf{y}_{2},\\ldots,\\mathbf{y}_{n}\\right]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times n}$, we write the risk as\nL87:  | $\\displaystyle\\widetilde{\\mathcal{R}}(\\bm{\\theta})=\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\mathbf{Y}\\|^{2},$  |  | (3)\nL88: \nL89: where $f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{X})=[f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{x}_{1}),\\ldots,f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{x}_{n})]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times n}$. We split the optimization (cite51†1 ) using gradient descent at step $t$ with learning rate $\\eta$ into two steps:\nL90:  | $\\displaystyle\\widetilde{\\bm{\\theta}}_{t}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\bm{\\theta}_{t-1}-\\eta\\,\\nabla\\widetilde{\\mathcal{R}}(\\bm{\\theta}_{t-1})^{\\top},$  |  | (4)\nL91:  | $\\displaystyle\\bm{\\theta}_{t}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\widetilde{\\bm{\\theta}}_{t}-\\lambda\\,\\eta\\left(\\widetilde{\\bm{\\theta}}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\right),$  |  | (5)\nL92: where the first step is the gradient descent on $\\widetilde{\\mathcal{R}}(\\bm{\\theta})$ and the second step is the gradient descent on the regularization term $\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\|\\bm{\\theta}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\|^{2}$.\nL93: ## 3 Proximity to the Pretrained Model Promotes Linearity\nL94: Figure 1: The NTK defines a linear function space $\\mathcal{H}$ tangent to the non-linear function space $\\mathcal{F}$ defined by the model. Regularized fine-tuning in the lazy regime is close to kernel regression on the tangent space. $f_{\\theta^{\\star}}(\\mathbf{x})$ is the fine-tuned model obtained by empirical risk minimization.\nL95: If fine-tuning remains in the linearized regime, then after $T$ steps of training $f_{\\theta^{\\star}}(\\mathbf{x})\\approx f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{x})+\\left\\langle\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}}f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{x}),\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}}_{T}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\right\\rangle$ is a good approximation.\nL96: We first justify why we regularize for proximity to the pretrained model by showing that this promotes similarity between the final fine-tuned solution and its linearized counterpart. For any $(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})\\in\\mathcal{D}_{T}$, the latter is defined as\nL97: \nL98:  | $\\displaystyle\\bar{f}_{\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}}_{t}}(\\mathbf{x})=f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{x})+\\left\\langle\\nabla f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{x}),\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\right\\rangle,$  |  | (6)\nL99: where $\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}}$ denotes the parameters of the linearized model. We set $\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}}_{0}=\\bm{\\theta}_{0}$ and, therefore, for any $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathcal{D}_{T}$, $\\bar{f}_{\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}}_{0}}(\\mathbf{x})=f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{x})$.\nL100: We list and discuss our main theorems toward this goal, and defer the proofs to the appendix. In Theorem cite52†1 , we provide conditions for monotonicity of updates in regularized fine-tuning, and use it to slightly modify gradient descent to ensure monotonicity. In Theorem cite53†2 , we derive an upper bound on the distance between the parameters at a step $t$ of the regularized fine-tuned model and the pretrained model, i.e., $\\|\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\|$.\nL101: In Theorem cite54†3 , we find the distance between the inference of the regularized fine-tuned model $f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{X})$ and the linearized model $\\bar{f}_{\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}}}(\\mathbf{X})$ i.e., $\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\bar{f}_{\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}}}(\\mathbf{X})\\|$, which is the primary contribution of this section.\nL102: ###### Theorem 1.\nL103: \nL104: Under the squared loss, for any $t>0$, if $\\lambda>0$ and $\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}}\\widetilde{\\mathcal{R}}(\\bm{\\theta}_{t})\\left(\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\right)\\geq 0$, then\nL105: \nL106:  | $$\\frac{d}{dt}\\|f_{\\theta_{t}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\mathbf{Y}\\|^{2}\\leq 0.$$  |  | (7)\nL107: \nL108: Moreover, if $\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}}\\widetilde{\\mathcal{R}}(\\bm{\\theta}_{t})\\left(\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\right)<0$, then $\\lambda=0$ is a sufficient condition for (cite55†7 ) to hold.\nL109: ###### Proof.\nL110: \nL111: See Appendix cite18†B . ∎\nL112: \nL113: In fine-tuning, the objective in (cite51†1 ) is non-convex in the parameters. However, according to Theorem cite52†1 , a sufficient condition for regularized fine-tuning to have non-increasing $\\|f_{\\theta_{t}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\mathbf{Y}\\|^{2}_{2}$, is that at step $t$, we have $\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}\\widetilde{\\mathcal{R}}(\\bm{\\theta}_{t})\\left(\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\right)\\geq 0$ or $\\lambda=0$. Therefore, we modify the regularization step in (cite56†5 ) as\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Statistical Framework for Alignment with Biased AI Feedback (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08259v1)\nciteturn31647view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view3\",\"lineno\":110}); Total lines: 458\nL96: where $r(X,Y)$ is a latent human reward function and $\\sigma(t)=\\{1+\\exp(-t)\\}^{-1}$ is the logistic function. Model (cite36†2.1 ) corresponds to the well-known Bradley–Terry preference model (cite29†Bradley and Terry, 1952 ) and has been widely implemented in the LLM training literature (cite28†Rafailov et al., 2023 ; cite25†Ouyang et al., 2022 ).\nL97: In RLHF with Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO, cite27†Schulman et al., 2017 ), one first fits a reward model $r_{\\phi}$ from preference data and then solves the KL-regularized control problem\nL98:  | $$\\max_{\\pi\\in\\Pi}\\Big\\{\\mathbb{E}_{X,Y\\sim\\pi(\\cdot\\mid X)}[r_{\\phi}(X,Y)]-\\beta\\,D_{\\mathrm{KL}}\\!\\big(\\pi(Y\\mid X)\\,\\|\\,\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y\\mid X)\\big)\\Big\\},$$  |  | (2.2)\nL99: \nL100: where $\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}$ is a fixed reference policy. $\\beta>0$ controls the regularization strength, and $\\Pi$ denotes the candidate LLM policy class.\nL101: Direct Preference Optimization (DPO; cite28†Rafailov et al., 2023 ) bypasses fitting a reward model by exploiting a closed-form characterization of the optimizer of (cite37†2.2 ) at the population level. Under standard regularity conditions, the optimal policy satisfies $\\pi(Y\\mid X)\\ \\propto\\ \\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y\\mid X)\\exp\\{\\beta^{-1}r_{\\phi}(X,Y)\\}$, which implies the reward–policy relation\nL102: \nL103:  | $$r_{\\phi}(X,Y)=\\beta\\log\\frac{\\pi(Y\\mid X)}{\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y\\mid X)}+\\beta\\log W(X),$$  |  | (2.3)\nL104: where $W(X)$ is a normalizing term and independent of $Y$. Substituting (cite38†2.3 ) into the Bradley–Terry likelihood yields the DPO objective\nL105: \nL106:  | $$L_{\\mathrm{DPO}}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}):=-\\mathbb{E}\\Bigg[\\log\\sigma\\Big(\\beta(2Z-1)\\big\\{\\Delta_{\\pi}(X;Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)})-\\Delta_{\\mathrm{ref}}(X;Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)})\\big\\}\\Big)\\Bigg],$$  |  | (2.4)\nL107: where $\\Delta_{\\pi}(X;Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)}):=\\log\\pi(Y^{(1)}\\mid X)-\\log\\pi(Y^{(2)}\\mid X)$ and $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{ref}}(X;Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)}):=\\log\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y^{(1)}\\mid X)-\\log\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y^{(2)}\\mid X)$. Essentially, DPO and PPO target the same population objective function under the BT model assumption (cite28†Rafailov et al., 2023 ). Identity Preference Optimization (IPO; cite30†Azar et al., 2024 ) provides an alternative preference-based formulation that does not rely on the BT model assumption.\nL108: At a high level, IPO seeks a policy that maximizes preference over a reference policy, subject to regularization:\nL109:  | $$\\max_{\\pi\\in\\Pi}\\ \\mathbb{P}\\big(\\pi\\succ\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\big)-\\beta D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(\\pi\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}})$$  |\nL110: \nL111: where $\\mathbb{P}(\\pi\\succ\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}})$ denotes the expected probability that, for a randomly drawn input $X$, a response sampled from policy $\\pi$ is preferred by a human over a response sampled from the reference policy $\\pi_{\\text{ref}}$. We use DPO and IPO as the two main alignment backbones for developing debiasing methods in Section cite10†3 .\nL112: ## 3 Debiasing Framework for Preference Alignment\nL113: \nL114: This section develops debiasing methods for preference alignment when AI-generated preferences systematically deviate from human preferences. We consider settings where a large AI-labeled dataset $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{AI}}$ is available, along with a smaller human-labeled dataset $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{Human}}$.\nL115: ### 3.1 Debiased Direct Preference Optimization\nL116: \nL117: Let $L_{\\mathrm{DPO}}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}})$ denote the population DPO objective in (cite39†2.4 ). For each data point, we define the DPO log-likelihood contribution as\nL118: \nL119:  | $$\\ell_{\\mathrm{DPO}}(\\pi;X,Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)},Z):=-\\log\\sigma\\!\\Big(\\beta(2Z-1)\\big\\{\\Delta_{\\pi}(X;Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)})-\\Delta_{\\mathrm{ref}}(X;Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)})\\big\\}\\Big).$$  |\nL120: \nL121: The goal of DPO is to learn an optimal policy\nL122:  | $$\\pi^{*}=\\arg\\min_{\\pi}\\mathbb{E}_{X\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{X},(Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)})\\sim\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{AI}}}\\ell_{\\mathrm{DPO}}(\\pi;X,Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)},Z).$$  |  | (3.1)\nL123: \nL124: Using the AI-labeled data $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{AI}}$, the empirical DPO loss is\nL125:  | $\\displaystyle\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DPO}}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}):$  | $\\displaystyle=-\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=n+1}^{n+N}\\log\\sigma\\Big(\\beta(2\\widehat{Z}_{i}-1)\\big\\{\\Delta_{\\pi}(X_{i};Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)})-\\Delta_{\\mathrm{ref}}(X_{i};Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)})\\big\\}\\Big)$  |\nL126:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=n+1}^{N}\\ell_{\\mathrm{DPO}}(\\pi;X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)},\\widehat{Z}_{i}).$  |\nL127: This objective is biased because $\\widehat{Z}_{i}$ is generated under the AI model $\\widehat{g}$ rather than the ground-truth mechanism $g$. We correct this bias using the human-labeled dataset. Moreover, since human-labeled pairs may be generated under a different response mechanism $\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}$ than the AI-generated data $\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{AI}}$, we further account for the resulting distributional shift by incorporating an appropriate density-ratio correction:\nL128:  | $$w(Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)}\\mid X):=\\frac{\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{AI}}(Y^{(1)}\\mid X)}{\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}(Y^{(1)}\\mid X)}\\cdot\\frac{\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{AI}}(Y^{(2)}\\mid X)}{\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}(Y^{(2)}\\mid X)}.$$  |  | (3.2)\nL129: If $\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}$ is unavailable, we estimate it from data and replace $w$ with an estimator $\\widehat{w}$ constructed from the estimated $\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}$. We employ the sample splitting technique so that $\\widehat{w}$ is independent of the evaluation sample (cite40†Cai et al., 2024 ; cite41†Cai et al., 2022 ). For notational simplicity, we write $w_{i}$ for either $w(\\cdot)$ or $\\widehat{w}(\\cdot)$ evaluated at $(X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)})$.\nL130: Thus, the bias induced by replacing $Z$ with $\\widehat{Z}$ can be estimated by the weighted difference:\nL131:  | $$\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DPO}}^{B}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}):=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}\\Big\\{\\ell_{\\mathrm{DPO}}(\\pi;X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)},\\widehat{Z}_{i})-\\ell_{\\mathrm{DPO}}(\\pi;X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)},Z_{i})\\Big\\},$$  |  | (3.3)\nL132: \nL133: where $\\widehat{Z}_{i}$ is generated from $(X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)})$ by the AI evaluator. The debiased loss is then\nL134:  | $$\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DDPO}}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}):=\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DPO}}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}})-\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DPO}}^{B}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}),\\qquad$$  |  | (3.4)\nL135: \nL136: and the estimated policy is defined as $\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{DDPO}}:=\\arg\\min_{\\pi\\in\\Pi}\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DDPO}}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}})$. The procedure is summarized in Algorithm cite42†1 .\nL137: \nL138: Algorithm 1 Debiased Direct Preference Optimization (DDPO)\nL139: 1: $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{Human}}=\\{(X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)},Z_{i})\\}_{i=1}^{n}$, $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{AI}}=\\{(X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)},\\widehat{Z}_{i})\\}_{i=n+1}^{n+N}$, reference policy $\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}$, KL weight $\\beta$.\nL140: \nL141: 2: Compute $w_{i}$ using (cite43†3.2 ) (or $w_{i}=\\widehat{w}_{i}$ via sample splitting).\nL142: \nL143: 3: Form $\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DPO}}^{B}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}})$ using (cite44†3.3 ).\nL144: 4: Minimize $\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DDPO}}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}})$ in (cite45†3.4 ) by stochastic gradient descent and estimate $\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{DDPO}}$.\nL145: \nL146: 5: return $\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{DDPO}}$.\nL147: The proposed debiasing method modifies the DPO objective function to remove the systematic bias introduced by AI evaluators. Our methodology is closely related to the prediction-powered inference (PPI) framework (cite46†Angelopoulos et al., 2023a ; cite47†Angelopoulos et al., 2023b ), which combines labeled data with auxiliary predictions from an external model to improve inference efficiency.\nL148: Unlike PPI, which is primarily designed for inference under the same data-generating distribution, our framework accommodates distributional shifts in the paired responses between AI and human preference data, and we account for this shift through the density-ratio weighting in (cite43†3.2 ).\n"
